Two things the record page will not tell you. Both were found on 2026-09-21 by someone
following the older wording of this paragraph and failing:

  * On Zenodo the file is called core2_writers_n10_100K.json. It is renamed here for
    readability. The bytes are unchanged — that is what the hash above shows.
  * Zenodo lists an MD5 beside each file, not a SHA-256. Comparing the value above against
    the one on the record page cannot match: they are different algorithms, and a reader who
    tries it learns nothing about this copy.

The record carries its own SHA256.txt beside the data. That is the file to compare against:

  curl -s https://zenodo.org/api/records/21773066/files/SHA256.txt/content

It lists one line per file in the record. The line ending in core2_writers_n10_100K.json must
carry exactly the hash printed above. If it differs, this copy has been altered and its
numbers should not be used.

(Record 21773066 is the version behind the concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21773065 above; Zenodo
resolves the concept DOI to the latest version, which is this one.)
